# sm_rev

Moddable C reimplementation of Super Metroid, built from disassembly. Runs the original game with a real SNES core (CPU/PPU/APU/DMA), while progressively replacing game logic with clean, hackable C. Physics, enemies, and level data are exposed as editable runtime structs — the goal is a "mod canvas" where any behavior can be tweaked without touching assembly.

## Features

- Full SNES core (`src/snes/`) — CPU, PPU, APU, DMA, SPC
- Game logic ported bank-by-bank from disassembly (`src/sm_*.c`)
- Hybrid dispatch: C takes over where ported, falls back to original ROM otherwise
- Hot-reload physics params via `sm.ini` — no recompile needed
- Multiple Samus instances, 2-player controls
- Python test harness with headless playback

## Build

### Linux

Requires SDL2.

```bash
make
make run
```

### macOS

Install SDL2 as a framework (download from [libsdl.org](https://libsdl.org) and drag to `/Library/Frameworks`), then:

```bash
make NATIVE_MAC=1
./sm_rev
```

`NATIVE_MAC=1` links against `SDL2.framework` directly and bundles the ROM into the binary — no separate asset files needed at runtime. It checks both `/Library/Frameworks` and `~/Library/Frameworks`.

### Bundle ROM into binary (any platform)

```bash
make BUNDLE_ASSETS=1 ROM_FILE=sm.smc CONFIG_FILE=sm.ini
```

### Windows

```bash
make  # uses sdl2-config --static-libs
```

## Sharing a macOS binary

Cross-compiling for macOS from Linux isn't practical. The standard approach is a GitHub Actions workflow with a `macos-latest` runner — it builds natively and uploads the result as a release artifact. See `.github/workflows/` if one exists, or open an issue.

## ROM

You need a Super Metroid ROM (`sm.smc`, US version). Not included. Place it in the project root before building, or use `BUNDLE_ASSETS=1` to embed it at compile time.

## Test

```bash
make test       # full suite
make test-fast  # fast subset
```

